add(id='ruwayda', n='Ruwayda (Al Ruwaida)', na='الرويضة', c='archaeological', s='Fortified coastal town of the 16th–18th centuries · Qatar Museums heritage site', lat=26.08, lng=51.15,
    p='Late Islamic', dr='c. 1500s – 1760s', el='Largest fort in Qatar · 2.25 ha with its outer wall', ic='wall', era='H', t0=526, t1=250,
    te='The fort that grew eight times', ta='القلعة التي كبرت ثماني مرات', se='A 2.5 km coastal town with the largest fort in Qatar, two mosques, a boatyard and warehouses, deserted when its people moved to Al Zubarah', sa='مدينة ساحلية بطول 2.5 كم تضم أكبر قلعة في قطر ومسجدين وورشة سفن ومخازن، هُجرت حين انتقل أهلها إلى الزبارة',
    desc="A ruined town on the north-west coast between Al Zubarah and Al Ruwais, stretching about 2.5 km along the shore, with a central square fort of two complexes covering about 2.25 ha including its outer wall. The fort was built in three phases, the last expansion making it eight times its original size; one solid corner tower about 4 m tall may have held a cannon. Discovered by Beatrice de Cardi's British mission in 1973, it was excavated in 2008–2014 by the University of Wales (Lampeter/Trinity Saint David) under Andrew Petersen with the Qatar Museums Authority, uncovering a seafront mosque rebuilt five times, an eight-room warehouse with a date press, a boat workshop, a well and a tomb. Finds include Iranian glazed pottery of the late 16th–early 17th century, 18th-century Chinese ceramics and a 5 cm bone plaque carved with an oryx, about 300 years old. Local informants told Lorimer the town was deserted around the 1760s when residents moved to Al Zubarah.",
    sig="The largest fort in Qatar and the best-studied of the pre-Zubarah coastal towns, showing a trading community whose pottery came from Iran and China.",
    ex='Beatrice de Cardi (1973); University of Wales Trinity Saint David (A. Petersen) with Qatar Museums Authority, 2008–2014',
    disc=['Fort of 2.25 ha, enlarged eight times over', 'Bone plaque carved with an oryx, c. 300 years old'],
    refs=['Petersen, A. et al. (2016). Ruwayda: an historic urban settlement in north Qatar. Post-Medieval Archaeology 50(2), 321–349.', 'Gillespie, F. (5 May 2013). An ancient settlement in north Qatar. Gulf Times.', 'Qatar Museums. Al Ruwaida Archaeological Site (heritage-sites page).'])

add(id='freiha', n='Freiha (Al Furayhah)', na='فريحة', c='archaeological', s='17th–18th-century fortified village north of Al Zubarah', lat=25.9953, lng=51.0264,
    p='Late Islamic', dr='17th century – 1937', el='Village of c. 50 ha · 700 m along the shore', ic='boat', era='H', t0=426, t1=89,
    te='The village before Zubarah', ta='القرية التي سبقت الزبارة', se='A fishing village with a fort, a mosque and 303 rock carvings, at its peak before its famous neighbour', sa='قرية صيد فيها قلعة ومسجد و303 نقوش صخرية، بلغت ذروتها قبل جارتها الشهيرة',
    desc="A deserted village on a shallow bay 3 km north of Al Zubarah, covering about 50 ha and extending some 700 m along the coast and 200 m inland. Excavations and documents put its peak in the 17th–18th centuries, probably before Zubarah; Lorimer counted about 150 inhabitants, mostly fishermen, in 1904, and it was occupied by Qatari forces in July 1937. Qal'at Freiha, a fortified building with corner towers, store rooms and date presses, was excavated and conserved in 2005; from 2009 the Qatar Islamic Archaeology and Heritage Project of Qatar Museums and the University of Copenhagen mapped the site and excavated the central mosque (mihrab, minbar, washing well and courtyard), houses and middens. Stone fishing weights, fish bones and tidal fish traps show a marine economy. Bibby and Glob recorded several hundred cup-marks 5–23 cm wide here in 1956, and Hans Kapel catalogued 303 carvings in 1983.",
    sig="A complete small coastal settlement of the pearling age, older than Al Zubarah, excavated as part of the World Heritage site's hinterland.",
    ex='Qatari archaeologists (2005); Qatar Museums and University of Copenhagen, QIAH project (A. Walmsley, T. Richter, G. Rees), 2009 onwards',
    disc=['Fort with date presses, conserved 2005', '303 rock carvings recorded in 1983'],
    refs=['Rees, G., al-Naimi, F., Richter, T., Bystron, A. & Walmsley, A. (2012). Archaeological excavations at the settlement of al-Furayhah (Freiha), north-west Qatar. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 42, 321–328.', 'Rees, G., Walmsley, A. & Richter, T. (2011). Investigations in the Zubarah hinterland at Murayr and Furayhah. PSAS 41, 309–316.', 'Wikipedia contributors (2026). Freiha, citing Lorimer (1904) and Qatar Museums (2016).'])

add(id='ain-mohammed', n='Ain Mohammed', na='عين محمد', c='archaeological', s='Early Islamic workshop settlement · Landscapes of Faith excavation', lat=26.0038, lng=51.0541,
    p='Early Islamic (Abbasid)', dr='c. 780 – 830 AD', el='24 structures · one of only 5 large clustered sites of the period', ic='jar', era='H', t0=1246, t1=1196,
    te='Abbasid workshops in the desert', ta='ورش عباسية في الصحراء', se='Paired rooms with plastered basins and platforms, probably for washing and dyeing wool 1,200 years ago', sa='غرف متجاورة بأحواض ومنصات مجصّصة، ربما لغسل الصوف وصبغه قبل 1,200 عام',
    desc="An abandoned village 1.5 km south-west of Freiha with 24 structures including two mosques, a fort and the masonry well that Lorimer recorded in 1908, and an Early Islamic site excavated since winter 2022 by the Qatar Museums Department of Archaeology's Landscapes of Faith project directed by Robert Carter. Occupation is dated by pottery and glass to about 780–830 AD. The main building was planned as three production units of paired rooms with gypsum-plastered basins and platforms, drains through the walls and no kitchens, resembling small industrial units; the team proposes textile work such as washing and dyeing wool. Most pottery was imported from southern Iraq, with wares from Iran, Bahrain, Oman and a few pieces from China; food remains include dates, wheat, barley, dugong and large quantities of pearl oysters. Qatar has more than 30 known sites of the 7th–9th centuries, but only five large clustered ones like Ain Mohammed and nearby Mesaika (c. 750–780 AD).",
    sig="Evidence that early Abbasid Qatar had purpose-built manufacturing settlements supplied from Iraq, not just villages.",
    ex='Qatar Museums Department of Archaeology, Landscapes of Faith project (R. Carter), 2022 onwards; open days for the public',
    disc=['Plastered basins and platforms in paired rooms', 'Pottery mainly from Abbasid Iraq, a few sherds from China'],
    refs=['Qatar Museums (2024). Landscapes of Faith: Project Summary (R. Carter).', 'The Peninsula (24 Feb 2026). Qatar Museums Archaeology Department announces open days at Ain Mohammed and Mesaika excavation sites.', 'Gulf Times (30 Nov 2023). QM invites public to visit Ain Mohammed archaeological site.'])

add(id='al-daasa', n="Al Da'asa", na='الدعاسة', c='archaeological', s='Largest Ubaid-period site in Qatar · west coast', lat=25.3694, lng=50.8089,
    p='Neolithic (Ubaid period)', dr='Neolithic, Ubaid period', el='Nearly 60 hearths', ic='flame', era='P', t0=7500, t1=6000,
    te='Sixty fires by the sea', ta='ستون موقدًا على البحر', se='A seasonal camp of fishers and hunters where painted Ubaid pottery from Mesopotamia was found in 1961', sa='مخيم موسمي لصيادين وجامعي ثمار عُثر فيه عام 1961 على فخار عبيدي مزخرف من بلاد الرافدين',
    desc="A Neolithic settlement on the west coast of Qatar, described as the most extensive Ubaid site in the country. It was discovered in 1961 by the Danish team of Holger Kapel, the largest of several flint sites they excavated in 1960–64, and a British team then identified the potsherds as Ubaid; Beatrice de Cardi's team worked here in 1978. Finds include many painted Ubaid sherds, quern fragments, flint scrapers, blades and arrowheads, a carnelian bead and three fragments of red non-Ubaid pottery, along with nearly sixty hearths and postholes for tents or huts. It is interpreted as a seasonal encampment of a hunting, fishing and gathering group that returned repeatedly; the stone tools match Arabian rather than Mesopotamian traditions. A village for Dukhan oil workers was founded on the spot in 1956.",
    sig="Qatar's principal link to the Ubaid world of southern Mesopotamia, showing seaborne contact across the Arabian Gulf in the Neolithic.",
    ex='Danish Archaeological Expedition (H. Kapel) 1961; B. de Cardi 1978',
    disc=['Painted Ubaid pottery identified after 1961', 'Nearly 60 hearths and tent postholes'],
    refs=["Wikipedia contributors (2026). Al Da'asa, citing Rice, M. (1994) The Archaeology of the Arabian Gulf and Abdul Nayeem, M. (1998) Qatar Prehistory and Protohistory.", 'Casey, P. & Vine, P. (1991). The Heritage of Qatar. Immel.'])

add(id='umm-al-maa', n='Umm Al Maa burial ground', na='أم الماء', c='archaeological', s='Cairn field of hundreds of tombs · Bronze Age to Sasanian', lat=25.8174, lng=50.9905,
    p='Bronze Age to Sasanian (dates debated)', dr='c. 3000 BC – 7th century AD', el='Mounds 3–6 m across · hundreds, possibly thousands', ic='mound', era='P', t0=5025, t1=1400,
    te='Mother of water, field of cairns', ta='أم الماء، حقل الركامات', se='Low stone mounds over graves spread across a well-watered depression, dug by Danish, Japanese and German teams', sa='ركامات حجرية منخفضة فوق قبور تنتشر في منخفض غني بالماء، نقّب فيها دنماركيون ويابانيون وألمان',
    desc="An archaeological area on the west coast about 80 km north-west of Doha, named for its freshwater wells; Lorimer in 1908 noted good masonry wells and a ruined fort built by a Shaikh of Bahrain. Most activity is concentrated in the Mleiha depression of about 25 km², where burial mounds 3–6 m in diameter and 10 cm to 1 m high are capped with limestone, many damaged by robbers, weathering and off-road driving. Bibby and Glob found 50 cairns in 1957, one with a stone cist, and dated pit-fired pottery and alabaster bowl sherds to about 3000 BC by comparison with Bahrain; de Cardi's team found Indian red polished ware and glazed pottery of the Sasanian period (3rd–7th century AD); a Japanese mission (Konishi, Gotoh, Akashi, 1988–91) excavated cists with beads, bronze rings and pottery and proposed a date around the 1st century BC–AD; a German survey led by Juergen Schreiber in 2008 estimated hundreds, possibly thousands, of tombs and saw them as the work of nomads.",
    sig="Qatar's largest ancient cemetery, whose contested dates span three millennia of nomadic life on the peninsula.",
    ex='Danish expedition (G. Bibby, P. Glob) 1957; B. de Cardi; Japanese mission 1988–91; German survey (J. Schreiber) 2008',
    disc=['50 cairns counted in 1957, hundreds or thousands by 2008', 'Sasanian Indian red polished ware'],
    refs=['Schreiber, J., Daroczi, T.-T., Muhle, B. & Ewersen, J. (2008/2009). Excavations at the burial ground of Umm al-Ma\', Qatar: preliminary report on the first season.', 'Abdul Nayeem, M. (1998). Qatar Prehistory and Protohistory from the Most Ancient Times. Hyderabad Publishers, 155–164.', 'Wikipedia contributors (2026). Umm Al Maa, citing Lorimer (1908) and Ferdinand (1993).'])

add(id='al-thaqab-fort', n='Al Thaqab Fort', na='قلعة الثقب', c='archaeological', s='Desert fort over a 35-foot well · 17th–19th century', lat=26.0325, lng=51.1164,
    p='Late Islamic', dr='Village from the 10th century · fort 17th – 19th century', el='Well 35 ft (11 m) deep', ic='well', era='H', t0=1100, t1=150,
    te='A fort to guard a well', ta='قلعة تحرس بئرًا', se='Four towers around a courtyard, built of coral and limestone where rainwater gathered in the wadi', sa='أربعة أبراج حول فناء، بُنيت من المرجان والحجر الجيري حيث يتجمع ماء المطر في الوادي',
    desc="A fort near the abandoned village of Ath Thaqab in Al Shamal, about 110 km from Doha and 4.8 km from Al Khuwayr. Its name comes from thaghab, the water that lies in channels after rain. The fort is square with three circular towers and one rectangular tower, a central courtyard with stairs, a bent entrance and buildings for storing and processing dates, built of rough coral and limestone blocks bonded with mud mortar; it is dated between the 17th and 19th centuries, while archaeological evidence suggests the village was inhabited from the 10th century. Lorimer in 1908 recorded a well about 35 feet (11 m) deep west of the fort whose good water was fetched by the people of Al Khuwayr. In July 1937 a force sent by Sheikh Abdullah Al Thani took the fort and confiscated 40 rifles from residents.",
    sig="One of the chain of northern desert forts built to protect wells, the scarcest resource of the pearling-age villages.",
    ex='Documented by J.G. Lorimer (1908); conserved and presented by Qatar Museums',
    disc=['Three round towers and one square tower', 'Well 35 ft deep used by neighbouring Al Khuwayr'],
    refs=['Haroon, A. (9 Apr 2019). The historic Al Thaqab Fort. Gulf Times.', 'Wikipedia contributors (2026). Ath Thaqab, citing Lorimer, J.G. (1908) Gazetteer and Qatar Digital Library files.'])

add(id='al-rekayat-fort', n='Al Rekayat Fort', na='قلعة الركيات', c='archaeological', s='Mid-18th-century well fort · ISESCO heritage list 2021', lat=26.0506, lng=51.1358,
    p='Late Islamic', dr='Mid-18th century (coins of 749–846 AD also found)', el='28 × 22 m', ic='wall', era='H', t0=280, t1=100,
    te='The fort named for wells', ta='القلعة التي سُميت باسم الآبار', se='A 28 by 22 m mud-and-limestone fort with a well inside, restored in 1988 and again in 2020–21', sa='قلعة من الطين والحجر الجيري بأبعاد 28 × 22 م وبداخلها بئر، رُممت عام 1988 ثم في 2020–2021',
    desc="A rectangular fort of mud and limestone, 28 × 22 m, on the road between Al Zubarah and Madinat Al Shamal about 110–115 km from Doha, with three rectangular corner towers and one cylindrical tower, narrow windowless rooms on three sides of a courtyard and a single entrance in the south wall. Its name refers to wells, and it was built to protect a freshwater well inside the fort for the surrounding village. Archaeological evidence suggests the mid-18th century, when Al Zubarah flourished, although coins of 749–846 AD were also found. First restored in 1988, it was conserved by Qatar Museums from October 2020 to September 2021, with a roof rebuilt in traditional danchal, basgil and manghrour, and in June 2021 it joined ISESCO's List of Heritage in the Islamic World.",
    sig="A well-preserved example of the water-guarding desert forts of northern Qatar, now one of three Qatari sites on the ISESCO heritage list.",
    ex='Qatar Museums Department of Architectural Conservation (A. al-Moslamani), 2020–2021; earlier restoration 1988',
    disc=['Freshwater well inside the walls', 'ISESCO Islamic World heritage list, June 2021'],
    refs=['Adly, A. (26 Mar 2026). Archeological sites a testimony to the rich history of Qatar\'s human activities. Gulf Times.', 'QNA / The Peninsula (21 Nov 2022). Al Rekayat Fort documents Qatar\'s history and civilisation.', 'Gulf Times (22 Feb 2022). Qatar Museums restores historic Al Rakiyat Fort in north-east Qatar.'])

