add(id='jebel-nakhsh', n='Jebel Nakhsh', na='جبل النخش', c='geological', s='33 km Miocene ridge · type area of the Dam Formation', lat=24.874, lng=50.9035,
    p='Dam Formation, Miocene', dr='Miocene', el='33 km long · c. 90 m high', ic='cliff', era='D', t0=23000000, t1=5000000,
    te='A ridge of gypsum and stromatolites', ta='ضلع من الجبس والستروماتوليت', se='The highest ground in southern Qatar, three rock layers of a Miocene sea, mined for gypsum in 1948', sa='أعلى أرض في جنوب قطر، ثلاث طبقات صخرية من بحر ميوسيني، استُخرج منها الجبس عام 1948',
    desc="A ridge in southern Qatar running 33 km from south-east to north-west parallel to the west coast, rising to about 90 m. It exposes three members of the Miocene Dam Formation: the Salwa Member (about 27 m) at the base, the gypsum-rich Nakhsh Member (about 33 m), the only member with stromatolites, and the Abu Samrah Member (about 6 m) on top. Weathering of soft clays has collapsed the lower limestone into dens used by foxes and hyenas, caves first surveyed by Peter Whybrow in 1983–84. After a 1947 survey, British authorities had the ridge mined for gypsum, with a railway to Umm Bab; output reached 650 tons in the second half of 1949, though geologist Jacques Leblanc notes the mountain shows no clear signs of exploitation.",
    sig="The best exposure of Qatar's Miocene sea-floor layers, and a landmark that shaped the geography of the south-west.",
    ex='P. Whybrow (1983–84); J. Leblanc, QatarEnergy; P. Andrews (2008) on the hyaena den',
    disc=['Nakhsh Member 33 m thick, rich in gypsum', 'Hyaena dens in collapsed limestone'],
    refs=['Leblanc, J. (2015). A Historical Account of the Stratigraphy of Qatar, Middle-East (1816 to 2015).', 'Andrews, P. (2008). Cetaceans from a possible striped hyaena den site in Qatar. Journal of Taphonomy 6(3–4).', 'Leblanc, J. (2009). A Fossil Hunting Guide to the Miocene of Qatar: Dam Formation.'])

add(id='dukhan-sabkha', n='Dukhan Sabkha', na='سبخة دخان', c='geological', s='Inland salt flat below sea level · Holocene evaporites', lat=25.32, lng=50.85,
    p='Holocene', dr='Last c. 5,000 years', el='107 km² basin · 1–2 m below sea level', ic='salt', era='D', t0=5000, t1=0,
    te='A sea that dried into salt', ta='بحر جفّ فصار ملحًا', se='A 107 km² hollow between the Dukhan anticline and the Qatar Arch where gypsum, halite and anhydrite still crystallise', sa='منخفض مساحته 107 كم² بين طيّة دخان المحدبة وقوس قطر، ما يزال يتبلور فيه الجبس والهاليت والأنهيدريت',
    desc="An inland sabkha on the west coast south of Dukhan, filling a synclinal depression between the Dukhan anticline to the west and the Qatar Arch to the east. Holocene sediments cover a structural low of 107 km², mostly 1–2 m below sea level, 4–6 m thick and up to 12 m, over Eocene carbonates; a 12 km² salt flat in the north-east is the youngest lake deposit. Gypsum is the dominant mineral (20–30%), with halite 8–11% and anhydrite about 5%, and prismatic gypsum up to 7 m thick records a saline lake at the centre. Sea-level reconstruction shows an early Holocene marine embayment flooding from the north that became ever more restricted into evaporite lakes; Qatar University work dates the sabkha to the last 5,000 years. Qatar has 78 inland sabkha patches covering about 205 km² and 42 coastal sabkhas totalling 701 km².",
    sig="A natural laboratory for how evaporites form, studied by Bristol and ExxonMobil Research Qatar as a model for ancient salt-bearing rocks.",
    ex='University of Bristol and ExxonMobil Research Qatar (Didi-Ooi, Whitaker, Jameson, Strohmenger), 2014–2015; Qatar University Science Journal',
    disc=['Gypsum beds up to 7 m thick', 'Water table 0.5–1 m down, rising and falling 40 cm a year'],
    refs=['Didi-Ooi, S.M., Whitaker, F., Simmonds, J., Jameson, J. & Strohmenger, C.J. (2015). Evaporite diagenesis models of a continental sabkha system: insights from the Holocene at Dukhan, Qatar. AAPG ACE, Denver.', 'Qatar University Science Journal. Geologic evolution of the Dukhan salt flats in western Qatar Peninsula, Arabian Gulf.', 'James, B. (5 Mar 2011). Call to highlight potential of hyper-saline coastal flats. Gulf Times.'])

add(id='mesaieed-sabkha', n='Mesaieed Coastal Sabkha', na='سبخة مسيعيد', c='geological', s='Largest coastal salt flat in Qatar · 55 km long', lat=24.85, lng=51.45,
    p='Holocene', dr='Last c. 6,000 years', el='55 km long · 4–14 km wide', ic='salt', era='D', t0=6000, t1=0,
    te='A lagoon filled with sand', ta='بحيرة شاطئية ملأتها الرمال', se='A former lagoon south of Mesaieed, now a gypsum-cemented plain that grew seaward over six thousand years', sa='بحيرة شاطئية قديمة جنوب مسيعيد صارت سهلًا متماسكًا بالجبس تقدّم نحو البحر على مدى ستة آلاف عام',
    desc="The largest coastal sabkha in Qatar, on the east coast just south of Mesaieed, roughly 55 km long and 4–14 km wide, formed from a former lagoon that filled with wind-blown siliciclastic sand. Bristol and ExxonMobil Research Qatar geologists describe it as a prograding coastal plain 4–6 km wide whose Holocene wedge is 3–6 m thick, reaching about 15 m at the coast, over Eocene limestones and dolomites. Gypsum is the main diagenetic mineral, 20–50% of the sediment over several km², with calcite, dolomite, anhydrite and halite minor; gypsum cement is pervasive in the inland part (about 6,000 years old), restricted to crusts in the centre (about 4,000 years) and largely absent at the coast (about 2,000 years). Evaporative pumping of groundwater from the Eocene aquifer is the main source of salts, and the gypsum helps stabilise a surface that vehicles can cross when dry.",
    sig="A textbook example of a prograding coastal sabkha, showing how a shoreline moved seaward and cemented itself with gypsum.",
    ex='University of Bristol and ExxonMobil Research Qatar (Didi-Ooi, Whitaker, Jameson, Strohmenger), 2014; UNESCO Sabkha Ecosystems volume (B. Boer)',
    disc=['Gypsum 20–50% of the sediment', 'Three age zones: 6,000, 4,000 and 2,000 years'],
    refs=['Didi-Ooi, S.M., Whitaker, F., Jameson, J. & Strohmenger, C.J. (2014). Mesaieed sabkha, southeast Qatar: evaporite diagenesis in a prograding coastal plain. AAPG ACE, Houston.', 'James, B. (5 Mar 2011). Call to highlight potential of hyper-saline coastal flats. Gulf Times (on the Sabkha Ecosystems series).'])

add(id='dahl-al-hamam', n='Dahl Al Hamam', na='دحل الحمام', c='geological', s='Collapse sinkhole in a Doha park · tidal pool inside', lat=25.3344, lng=51.4805,
    p='Karst in Eocene Rus and Dammam formations', dr='Middle Pleistocene to present', el='60 m long · entrance 12 m wide, 9 m high', ic='sinkhole', era='D', t0=700000, t1=0,
    te='A cave in the middle of the city', ta='كهف في قلب المدينة', se='A collapse doline whose brackish pool rises and falls 30 cm with the tide, one of more than 9,700 depressions in Qatar', sa='حفرة انهيارية ترتفع بركتها المالحة وتنخفض 30 سم مع المد والجزر، واحدة من أكثر من 9,700 منخفض في قطر',
    desc="A collapse doline, or sinkhole, fenced in the north-east corner of Dahl Al Hamam Park in central Doha, about 60 m long with a portal 12 m wide and 9 m high and a staircase of about 20 steps down the cliff. Brackish water at its sides rises and falls about 30 cm with the tides, suggesting a connection to the sea; divers explored underwater passages behind the pool in 2016, and a 2016 gravity survey by Howari, Aldouri and Sadiq found a strong negative anomaly marking water-filled caves linked to the Arabian Gulf. Lorimer in 1908 called it Dahail and noted a rocky pool of good water. It belongs to a karst landscape of more than 9,700 depressions in Qatar, formed by subsurface dissolution of limestone, dolomite, gypsum and anhydrite of the Eocene Rus and Dammam formations under wet Middle Pleistocene conditions, followed by subsidence. Pigeons nesting inside gave the cave, and the district, its name.",
    sig="The most accessible of Qatar's dahl caves, and a reminder that the capital stands on dissolving Eocene rock.",
    ex='Sadiq & Nasir (2002) karst study; Howari, Aldouri & Sadiq (2016) gravity survey; Doha municipality park',
    disc=['Pool that moves with the tide', 'More than 9,700 karst depressions in Qatar'],
    refs=['Sadiq, A.M. & Nasir, S.J. (2002). Middle Pleistocene karst evolution in the State of Qatar, Arabian Gulf. Journal of Cave and Karst Studies 64(2), 132–139.', 'Howari, F.M., Aldouri, R. & Sadiq, A. (2016). Gravity investigations of recent sinkholes and karst pits of Dahal Al-Hamam, State of Qatar. Environmental Earth Sciences 75(5), 440.', 'Showcaves.com. Dahl Al Hamam (2025).'])

