add(id='al-shahaniya-oryx-reserve', n='Al Shahaniya Oryx Reserve', na='محمية الشحانية', c='ecology', s='Qatar\'s first Arabian oryx sanctuary · 1979', lat=25.37, lng=51.22,
    p='Modern conservation', dr='1979 – present', el='12 km² · 45 km from Doha', ic='gazelle', era='N', t0=47, t1=0,
    te='Where the white oryx came back', ta='حيث عاد المها الأبيض', se='A 12 km² reserve founded in 1979 for the national animal, declared extinct in the wild in 1972', sa='محمية مساحتها 12 كم² أُسست عام 1979 للحيوان الوطني الذي أُعلن انقراضه في البرية عام 1972',
    desc="A 12 km² reserve at Al Shahaniya, about 45 km from Doha, designated in 1979 as a sanctuary for the Arabian oryx and one of the first nature reserves established by the State. The oryx were brought from Muaither farm by Sheikh Abdul Rahman bin Saud Al Thani, and Sheikh Jassim bin Hamad Al Thani was the first to give the animals special attention there; the reserve also holds a pen for North African red-necked ostriches. The species had been declared extinct in the wild in October 1972 after poachers killed the last herd in Oman; Sheikh Faisal bin Qassim Al Thani's 1967 expedition caught five animals in Oman that founded Qatar's Royal Herd. The IUCN moved the oryx from Endangered to Vulnerable in 2011, and the animals have since spread to more than 10 protected areas in Qatar, with up to 10,000 wild and semi-wild oryx estimated in the country.",
    sig="The starting point of Qatar's oryx recovery, one of the Gulf's most successful returns of an animal from extinction in the wild.",
    ex='Ministry of Environment and Climate Change, Natural Reserves Department; Qatari royal herds since 1967',
    disc=['Founded 1979, 12 km²', 'Oryx now in more than 10 protected areas'],
    refs=['Qatar News Agency (15 Apr 2025). Qatar\'s nature reserves: promoting ecosystem sustainability and wildlife preservation.', 'eNature Qatar (Ministry of Environment and Climate Change). Al Sheehaniya Reserve.', 'Payne, S. (5 Jan 2025). The Arabian oryx: how Qatar\'s national animal came back from the dead. BBC Discover Wildlife.'])

add(id='al-mashabiya-reserve', n='Al Mashabiya Reserve', na='محمية المسحبية', c='ecology', s='54 km² oryx and gazelle reserve in the south-west · 1997', lat=24.72, lng=50.95,
    p='Modern conservation', dr='1997 – present', el='c. 54 km² · c. 1,000 oryx', ic='gazelle', era='N', t0=29, t1=0,
    te='A thousand oryx among the dunes', ta='ألف مها بين الكثبان', se='Sandy plains south-east of Abu Samra where oryx and reem gazelles are bred for release', sa='سهول رملية جنوب شرق أبو سمرة تُربّى فيها المها وغزلان الريم لإطلاقها',
    desc="A reserve of about 54 km² in south-west Qatar, south-east of Abu Samra, officially opened in 1997 to resettle the Arabian oryx, the reem (sand) gazelle and other endangered animals. Its terrain is sandy plains with hills and valleys where natural grasses, seasonal herbs and wild trees grow among the dunes. According to its manager Mohammed Nasser Al Henzab, quoted in January 2025, the reserve holds around 1,000 oryx. It is one of 12 terrestrial and marine reserves that, with Umm Al Sheif approved at the end of 2024, cover about 25.73% of Qatar's territory, with a national goal of 30% by 2030 under the 2004 wildlife protection decree.",
    sig="The largest single oryx herd reported in Qatar, and the breeding base for the country's gazelle and oryx reintroductions.",
    ex='Ministry of Environment and Climate Change, Natural Reserves Department (S.H. Al Kuwari); reserve manager M.N. Al Henzab',
    disc=['About 1,000 oryx reported in 2025', 'Reserves cover 25.73% of Qatar'],
    refs=['Qatar News Agency (15 Apr 2025). Qatar\'s nature reserves: promoting ecosystem sustainability and wildlife preservation.', 'eNature Qatar (Ministry of Environment and Climate Change). Al Mashabiya Reserve.', 'Payne, S. (5 Jan 2025). The Arabian oryx: how Qatar\'s national animal came back from the dead. BBC Discover Wildlife.'])

add(id='irkaya-farm', n='Irkaya Farm and Reserve', na='محمية مزرعة إركية', c='ecology', s='Qatar\'s richest birding site · 238 species recorded', lat=25.01, lng=51.18,
    p='Modern farmland oasis', dr='Late 20th century – present', el='238 of Qatar\'s 315 bird species', ic='bird', era='N', t0=50, t1=0,
    te='Fodder fields full of birds', ta='حقول علف تعجّ بالطيور', se='Irrigated pivot fields on the Abu Samra road where 76% of all birds ever recorded in Qatar have been seen', sa='حقول محورية مروية على طريق أبو سمرة شوهد فيها 76% من كل الطيور المسجلة في قطر',
    desc="A fodder farm off the Abu Samra Road about 50 km west of Doha, in the south-central plain, watered by central-pivot irrigation on a flat sandy plain. By 2014, when a book on its fauna and flora was launched at the Ministry of Environment, 238 of the 315 bird species recorded in Qatar (76%) had been seen at Irkaya, more than anywhere else in the country, including 15 globally threatened species such as the sociable lapwing, and many of Qatar's 59 breeding species nest there. Native plants near the pivot run-off grow taller with larger leaves than in the open desert; birders report close to 250 species and a southern farm of roughly 15 km² with lagoons, reed beds and grassland. The Ministry now lists Erkia as a nature reserve on the migratory route of birds and insects, with a farm growing animal feed inside it.",
    sig="Proof that a single irrigated farm can become the most important stopover for migratory birds on the peninsula.",
    ex='Ministry of Environment and Climate Change (Erkia Reserve); Qatar birdwatchers who compiled the 2014 Irkaya book',
    disc=['15 globally threatened bird species recorded', 'Sociable lapwing, one of the world\'s rarest waders'],
    refs=['The Peninsula (10 Mar 2014). Fauna, flora of Irkaya farm.', 'Qatar News Agency (15 Apr 2025). Qatar\'s nature reserves: promoting ecosystem sustainability and wildlife preservation (Erkia Reserve).', 'Birdingplaces.eu. Irakhiya Farm, Qatar (site guide).'])

add(id='abu-nakhla-wetland', n='Abu Nakhla Wetland', na='بحيرة أبو نخلة', c='ecology', s='Artificial wetland of 9 km² south of Doha · flamingo flocks', lat=25.19, lng=51.42,
    p='Modern', dr='1982 – present', el='9 km² · 12 km from Doha', ic='bird', era='N', t0=44, t1=0,
    te='A lake made of waste water', ta='بحيرة من ماء الصرف المعالج', se='A treated-sewage reservoir since 1982 that became Qatar\'s biggest wetland and a flamingo haunt, then faced draining', sa='خزان لمياه الصرف المعالجة منذ عام 1982 صار أكبر أرض رطبة في قطر ومقصدًا للنحام، ثم واجه التجفيف',
    desc="An artificial wetland with natural topography about 12 km south of Doha near Abu Nakhla village, created in 1982 as a reservoir for treated sewage effluent from the Doha-South and Doha-West treatment stations, spread over nine square kilometres. At least 260 bird species have been recorded, with about 150 greater flamingos counted, frogs and fish in the pond, reeds and cat's-tail around it, and about 10% of Qatar's documented plant species in the area; the pond sits on the Simsima/Umm Bab and Midra Shale members of the Eocene Dammam Formation over a shallow aquifer, and a 2014–2015 study found low bacterial contamination and acceptable heavy-metal levels. In March 2014 the Public Works Authority Ashghal, under its president Nasser bin Ali al-Mawlawi, announced the lagoon would be drained by the end of that year; Qatar University's Anna Grichting Solder, who studied the site with her students, and Saif al-Hajri of Friends of the Environment argued in 2015 for an ecological wetland reserve with bio-drainage and bioremediation, noting the Ramsar secretariat and BirdLife International were ready to support a Ramsar listing, as Abu Dhabi's Al Wathba had received in 2013.",
    sig="The largest wetland in Qatar, an accidental ecosystem that raised the question of what a desert city owes the birds it attracted.",
    ex='Qatar University Department of Architecture and Urban Planning (A. Grichting Solder); Friends of the Environment (S. al-Hajri)',
    disc=['At least 260 bird species, c. 150 greater flamingos', 'Drainage plan announced in 2014'],
    refs=['Holla, A. (25 Aug 2015). A case for Qatar\'s biggest wetland. Gulf Times.', 'Gulf Times (6 Mar 2014). Abu Nakhla lagoon to be drained by end of this year.', 'Elobaid, E.A., Sadooni, F.N., Lebbe, L. et al. (2018). Environmental evaluation, risk assessment and management options of Abu Nakhla treated sewage effluent unlined pond. Qatar Foundation Annual Research Conference Proceedings.'])

add(id='al-aaliya-island', n='Al Aaliya Island', na='جزيرة العالية', c='ecology', s='Seabird colony island off Doha · Important Bird Area', lat=25.4064, lng=51.5656,
    p='Modern', dr='Present', el='2 km sandspit at low tide', ic='island', era='N', t0=100, t1=0,
    te='Cormorants within sight of the towers', ta='غاقات على مرمى من الأبراج', se='A low coral-reef island north of The Pearl where Socotra cormorants and white-cheeked terns breed', sa='جزيرة مرجانية منخفضة شمال اللؤلؤة يتكاثر فيها غاق سقطرى والخرشنة بيضاء الخد',
    desc="A small, low-lying island off Al Daayen municipality north of The Pearl, 3.5 miles north of Al Safliya Island, with a brown surface and a small peak at its east end that once guided ships into Al Bidda harbour. Unlike Halul, which rests on ancient igneous and metamorphic rock, it is built of coral-reef structures, with loose weathered limestone, scattered Limonium and Zygophyllum bushes, a 2 km sandspit extending south at low tide and broad intertidal flats and coral reefs around it. BirdLife International designated it an Important Bird Area for its breeding colonies of Socotra cormorants and white-cheeked terns. The artificial island of The Pearl, built in the late 2000s, now lies between Al Aaliya and Al Safliya.",
    sig="A breeding colony of the Gulf-endemic Socotra cormorant a few kilometres from Doha's skyline.",
    ex='BirdLife International (Important Bird Area / Key Biodiversity Area); Centre for GIS of Qatar',
    disc=['Socotra cormorant and white-cheeked tern colonies', 'Coral-reef island, not bedrock'],
    refs=['BirdLife International (2024). Important Bird Area factsheet: Al-Aliyah island, Qatar.', 'Wikipedia contributors (2026). Al Aaliya Island, citing the British Hydrographic Department Gulf Pilot (1890) and BirdLife Data Zone (2024).'])

add(id='umm-tais-national-park', n='Umm Tais National Park', na='حديقة أم تيس الوطنية', c='ecology', s='Island park at Qatar\'s northern tip · hawksbill nesting', lat=26.12, lng=51.30,
    p='Modern conservation', dr='2006 – present', el='One of 8 main turtle nesting sites · 219 turtles nested in 2025', ic='mangrove', era='N', t0=20, t1=0,
    te='Mangroves at the top of the map', ta='قرم في أقصى الشمال', se='Sand bars, islets and mangroves on an uninhabited island declared a national park in 2006', sa='حواجز رملية وجزر صغيرة وقرم على جزيرة غير مأهولة أُعلنت حديقة وطنية عام 2006',
    desc="A national park on an uninhabited island at the northern tip of Qatar, within 6.4 km of Ar Ruwais, established in 2006 during the 15th Asian Games. It has sand bars and small islets with mangroves, is a stopover for migratory birds on the Afro-Eurasian flyway and a nursery ground for fish and crustaceans, with dugong sightings in surrounding waters, and is one of the main hawksbill sea turtle nesting sites in the country. In the 2025 season, from 31 March to the end of July, the Ministry of Environment and Climate Change with QatarEnergy and Qatar University's Environmental Science Center recorded 219 nesting turtles across eight main sites including Umm Tais, Fuwairit, Ras Rakan, Ras Laffan and Al Ghariya, and released 8,213 hatchlings, bringing the project's five-year total to more than 60,000. The ruins of the old village of Al Mafjar lie nearby.",
    sig="The northernmost protected area of the peninsula, joining mangroves, seabirds and hawksbill turtles in one small island.",
    ex='Ministry of Environment and Climate Change, Wildlife Development Department; QatarEnergy; Qatar University Environmental Science Center',
    disc=['National park since 2006', '8,213 hawksbill hatchlings released nationwide in 2025'],
    refs=['Qatar News Agency (2 Aug 2025). MoECC announces conclusion of 2025 nesting season for hawksbill sea turtles.', 'Wikipedia contributors (2026). Umm Tais National Park, citing Marhaba (2015) and Orr, T. (2008) Qatar (Cultures of the World).', 'Plizio Visual Lab. Umm Tais National Park, Qatar.'])

